    {
      "id": "va-O364469",
      "museum": "Victoria and Albert Museum",
      "source": "https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O364469/",
      "title": "Dress",
      "accession": "T.250-1923",
      "description": "This dress is from Bethlehem, Palestine, dating from 1890-1920. It is an example of Palestinian tatreez - elaborate hand-embroidery on clothing and accessories - a practice and craft which was characterised by remarkable complexity and regional diversity across Palestine in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. The base material is woven cotton, indigo-dyed, with woven bands of yellow and deep red along the sleeves and sides of the garment. At the back lower panel of the dress, fabric woven with metallic threads has been included, a gesture typical of Bethlehem dresses of this style. The chest panel has been left unembroidered in the centre, with a variety of border stitches around the sides. The yoke is of a foreign silk fabric, used in this period as a nod to fashion and a mark of status.",
      "placesOfOrigin": [
        {
          "place": {
